 "text": "A shoebox of cash gained power in 1932. In 2026, the same math runs the other way. From September 1929 to July 1932, consumer prices fell by about 21%. So the same $1,000 now buys what about $1,270 bought in 1929. Twelve months later, U.S. consumer prices are 3.7% higher. That's the official number for the year to September 2026. So the $1,000 now buys what about $964 bought a year earlier. They just buy less. Same box. Opposite result. In the early 1930s, cash gained power because prices were falling. In 2026, prices are rising, so the same math runs the other way. That's arithmetic, not a plan.",
